# glm-router settings (copy to ~/glm-router/glm-router.env and edit; every address and path here is a placeholder).
# The router binary: vllm-router 0.1.17 (vllm-project/router 3451e66) with this directory's three patches, see
# build-router.sh. The pip wheel's launcher cannot take --api-key-validation-urls, so it has no inbound auth at all:
# test use only.
ROUTER_BIN=$HOME/glm-router/bin/vllm-router
# Where it listens. 127.0.0.1 for tests; production needs the address your ingress reaches, and then BIND_APPROVED=1
# (exposing the router is the operator's decision) and inbound auth (VALIDATE=1, below).
BIND=127.0.0.1
BIND_APPROVED=0
PORT=8602
METRICS_PORT=8603
# The workers: one serve per Galaxy, one URL per line in WORKERS_FILE (the start list; add/remove/drain keep it in
# step with the running router). Example lines: http://<galaxy-1-address>:8000
WORKERS_FILE=$HOME/glm-router/workers
# Inbound auth: each request's bearer key is checked once (then cached) by a GET of the workers' /v1/models, which
# serve answers 200 only with its API key; the router sends no key of its own, so the client's key goes on to the
# serve, which checks it again. Every route, the admin API and /health then need the key.
VALIDATE=1
# Optional: the URLs that validate keys (space-separated); default the start list's /v1/models. List every Galaxy's
# serve here when the router may start with one worker (a rolling swap). Production lists all four.
#VALIDATION_URLS="http://<galaxy-1-address>:8000/v1/models http://<galaxy-2-address>:8000/v1/models http://<galaxy-3-address>:8000/v1/models http://<galaxy-4-address>:8000/v1/models"
# cache_aware as production runs it: threshold 0.3, abs = slots per worker (4 groups x 2 users), rel 2.
SLOTS_PER_WORKER=8
CACHE_THRESHOLD=0.3
BALANCE_REL=2
# Router retries of a retryable status (5 attempts, 50 ms x 1.5 by default).
RETRIES=5
LOGDIR=$HOME/glm-router/logs
# The serve API key, for check/drain (read from this file, never printed).
KEY_FILE=$HOME/glm-router/api-key
# check: alert command on a failure (called with one message argument), and the share of cache_aware decisions
# without token ids (render failures: the router then routes least-loaded) that counts as a failure.
ALERT_CMD=
MISSING_TOKENS_MAX=0.05
# The four switches of the patched router; production sets all four. Each is off when unset, and with all four off
# the binary routes exactly as stock 0.1.17.
# Media placeholders (router-media-placeholders.patch): cache_aware keys a chat with images by a placeholder per
# image, so a conversation with images stays on the Galaxy that holds its cache.
VLLM_ROUTER_MEDIA_PLACEHOLDERS=1
# Random ties (same patch): least-loaded ties are broken at random instead of always going to the first worker.
VLLM_ROUTER_RANDOM_TIES=1
# Retry-exclude (router-retry-exclude.patch): a retry never goes back to a worker that already answered this request
# with a retryable status (429, 408, 500, 502, 503, 504) while another worker is left; with every worker excluded it
# picks from all again. Keep the admission cap in front of the router at or below 75% of the total slots with it on:
# above capacity it moves turns away from the Galaxy that holds their cache.
VLLM_ROUTER_RETRY_EXCLUDE_FAILED=1
# Port sharing (router-reuseport.patch): REUSEPORT=1 runs the router with SO_REUSEPORT on PORT (the script exports
# VLLM_ROUTER_REUSEPORT=1), so `glm-router.sh handoff` starts the next router beside it with no refused connection.
# The handoff's router takes the other metrics port (METRICS_PORT_B, default METRICS_PORT + 1); they alternate.
REUSEPORT=1
METRICS_PORT_B=8604
# The tmux session name (the handoff's router runs in INSTANCE-b). A second instance on the same host needs its own
# copy of this file with its own INSTANCE, PORT, METRICS_PORT, METRICS_PORT_B, LOGDIR and WORKERS_FILE.
# `glm-router.sh metrics` prints the live metrics port.
INSTANCE=glm-router
